Hey All,

I have a question about the experimental cross population that I'm trying to use. The goal is to track down the QTL responsible for the (orange) color pattern in these animals. The issue is that our organism has relatively low survival when crossed, which results in smaller broods. I've made a flow-chart that should help explain the issue:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/--PequIfcz0I/WrKqLv4zUgI/AAAAAAAAZwk/G5zyj5O_vVc7EETRpGo6y0yX-6Q-B8gTgCLcBGAs/s1600/RAD%2Bspecimens%2Bflow%2Bchart.jpg>
Essentially what I'm wondering is:
I think that I should be able to combine family 1's F2's no problem. So I'll have one big brood of 36 individuals from that. Is that correct?
Family 2 looks like a weird backcross, but it's actually pretty standard. It's not really using the parental black butterflies for the second-matings, is all. So can I do the same thing as in #1?
Can I combine, say, family 1 and 3. It would seem that that would be problematic unless all that I'm doing is something like a BSA. A mapping analysis seems like it might have too much trouble with that because of parental variation, right?
Finally, can I do the maps for Family 1, 2, 3, and 4 separately and then do something to combine them afterwards?
So far I've got my data (1000's of biallelic loci for each individual, not perfect coverage, not always informative) loaded into r/qtl and I've been able to run scanone() on the individual broods.

Any advice or direction would be greatly appreciated!

Best,
Evan

